  <h3>The Edge, Man</h3>=0A<p>Ryan Mulligan=E2=80=99s article <a href=3D"ht=
tps://post.spmailtechnol.com/f/a/WrnGbyAn3IjWCv6D24mdFw~~/AABEfhA~/RsCefxbo=
FeTLeREAtYn7RclsOPbkUjy3C0Bb-zU9Jz_mwhOPx-br82iSZJU3memYZcnBIK2TB3C-bhc0Hg-=
QU7T4XmuqaQRmndX9Qbd3kUaWYACASNTj4KQu6InLht8hKqL1hCTYhYpK0LIaoJB8EPY83pTEBX=
bgu29CFkrGXP0~">Transition to the Other Wide with Container Query Units</a>=
 is some solid CSS catnip for me. Just this base thought is fun to think ab=
out: how do you move an element from flush-left to flush-right when the siz=
e of the container and element are both unknown (which is typical).</p>=0A<=
img src=3D"https://production-codepen-email-assets.codepenassets.com/cdn-cg=
i/image/width=3D600,quality=3D90,format=3Dauto/codepen-spark/edge-to-edge-m=
ulligan.png" alt=3D"" width=3D"400"/>=0A<p>It=E2=80=99s like you want to ha=
ve <code>left: 0;</code> as a starting state and <code>right: 0;</code> as =
an ending state, but as those a different properties, you cannot animate be=
tween them. You could have <code>left: 0;</code> as the starting state (or =
nothing, as that=E2=80=99s default) and <code>left: 100%;</code> as the end=
ing state, but that=E2=80=99s not =E2=80=9Cflush right=E2=80=9D, it=E2=80=
=99s =E2=80=9Calign the left edge of the element with the right edge of the=
 container=E2=80=9D, which isn=E2=80=99t quite right. A good fix for this i=
s to make <code>left: 100%; translate: -100% 0;</code> the ending state! Ki=
nd of a classic. The <code>-100%</code> of the translate refers to the widt=
h of the element, so it sucks it back to the left =E2=80=9Cone element=E2=
=80=99s worth=E2=80=9D, which ends up with flush-right alignment. Cool.</p>=
=0A<p>That solution is maybe a little funky though. Personally, I don=E2=80=
=99t love how there is a use of two =E2=80=9C100%=E2=80=9D values that just=
-so-happen to mean different things, and they are sitting right next to eac=
h other. Ryan presents a bit of a better solution in making the container a=
 <em>literal</em> container with <code>container-type: inline-size;</code> =
so you can use container units inside, then moving the element with <code>t=
ranslate: calc(100cqi - 100%);</code>. The different units there, and the s=
ingle declaration, do feel better to me.</p>=0A<p>That=E2=80=99s kinda wher=
e Ryan stopped, but he did mention View Transitions at the top of the post,=
 and that=E2=80=99s interesting to me because of <em>how</em> I opened the =
2nd paragraph above. View Transitions don=E2=80=99t really care how you mov=
e something from one state to another, it just looks at the before and afte=
r states and tweens. So we really <em>could</em> do <code>left: 0;</code> a=
nd <code>right: 0;</code> as the two states and let the movement happen bet=
ween them. But now we=E2=80=99re talking JavaScript of course, so we=E2=80=
=99ve got to use JavaScript events. Tradeoffs all around!</p>=0A<p>While I =
was playing with it I put all three ideas <a href=3D"https://post.spmailtec=
hnol.com/f/a/vcyaPxCLcBCPtIkO_kafaQ~~/AABEfhA~/wrmHi4e7LYOoC5Ou5OwKpLXmFdWs=
54hmejmKullkMJ6e5iV0DolRSGS2hBefmVPbY5WWzOXSH1cTz_oonG1mf8thW6Tt906S5nzzcBR=
VR0vHhl1cyilRha8uXn8aqAMQgHsv1p8_xXQj_g-mKKzG0qUgG0C_l-1jZa7Ga0vBUeXxiTPEfc=
aI3HLL9JyVwuX6">into a Pen</a>, because, uhh, duh.</p>=0A<hr />=0A<p>While =
that kinda stuff is a <em>little</em> cutting edge, it=E2=80=99s not nearly=
 as much as the rest of these HOT LINKS I=E2=80=99ve saved for you.</p>=0A<=
p>There is a thing called <a href=3D"https://post.spmailtechnol.com/f/a/fYy=
6YPqlCRF6LXASqEbbbw~~/AABEfhA~/dzRyzjJUokBPcP3maS9r2BuN-Rr89Knxse47gsUjWbam=
sDDVYN4LGztc3GVD5g8Ob4JQrDcbvWGxqxfwCcn3INyWNCaTVLj-BUe2QdmbCJdo0dmswRNaAXz=
ICz4e4mdwjW1fHUWykQWYX3Qc7ABqBYEkIglnGLQ75qMJ4STaPuU~">Scroll-Triggered Ani=
mations</a> now. Scroll-Driven Animations are already very cool, but those =
attach a timeline to scrolling position in one way or another. With the =E2=
=80=9CTriggered=E2=80=9D varietal, the animation can keep a duration-based =
timeline, but just be kicked off when a particularly element becomes visibl=
e (which itself has lots of control points). I like looking at really dumb =
easy implementations to get it, so I made my own <a href=3D"https://post.sp=
mailtechnol.com/f/a/uiXt-o3YNGjvkc6JoemkwA~~/AABEfhA~/rN4bEstKqKUFTJ7uEpQoR=
PhdKo3XHgEoHyLOkZ4VduJVqxyDLx8AcKDpZSUaQVeIw1TpluhHgSfU3jApgm2iaHqlsDH6x0PT=
